          "content": "The town kept its clocks five minutes slow, not by mistake but by agreement. Long ago, a watchmaker had suggested that a little borrowed time might make people kinder. No one knew whether he was right, but the clocks stayed that way after his shop became a bakery last winter.\n\nVisitors often noticed the difference first at the station, where the train arrived exactly when the timetable promised, and five minutes before the platform clock admitted it. Children loved this. They claimed the town was a country that had escaped from the rest of the world for breakfast every day.\n\nMara, who delivered letters, understood the custom differently. She said the extra minutes belonged to nobody and therefore could be given away. She used them to help a neighbor carry groceries, to listen to a lonely story, or to stand under the chestnut tree and watch its leaves turn gold.\n\nOne autumn, the council proposed correcting the clocks. A consultant had explained that the arrangement caused confusion, and confusion cost money. At the meeting, the baker asked how much money. The consultant shuffled his papers and said the figure was difficult to calculate. Nobody seemed particularly surprised by this answer.\n\nIn the end, the council left the clocks alone. The train continued to arrive on time, the children continued to boast, and Mara continued to give away her minutes. When people asked whether the town had really gained anything, she smiled and pointed to the chestnut tree, still standing there.",
